{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 10–20 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model underestimates by ~+2 to +6 €/t, largest around days 5–20).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast captures direction but reacts too slowly to sharp rallies; steepen the early-horizon slope (front-load increases) rather than spreading gains evenly across 30 days.",
    "Mid-horizon underestimation strongest: errors are typically largest in the middle of the window (≈ day 7–18), so apply a hump-shaped uplift (bigger uplift mid-window, smaller at day 1 and day 30).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after peaks: once the true series rolls over after a rally, the model stays too high; apply stronger mean reversion beyond ~day 20 (pull forecasts down toward a rolling mean when recent volatility/spike is high).",
    "Spike handling: after a rapid run-up in the history, cap further upside in the forecast and increase probability/size of pullback in the last third of the horizon (model tends to extrapolate the elevated level too long)."
  ]
}
